{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the cloned-repo how-to at exact clean pushed head bf589838360a52e3aaac884611348f4eb84a0710, under planner request 2dc880af, and file a verdict naming both artifacts.\n\nBASES CHECKED AT WRITE TIME. 2dc880af is live, unstale, addressed to me, commitment open. Both cited artifacts stand at exactly that head and are live and unstale: 8a0d7203 at docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md, 558df0f2 at docs/README.md. Planner authored both and I authored neither, so independence holds. The head exists locally.\n\nWHY THIS ONE DESERVES CARE RATHER THAN A DOCUMENTATION SKIM. This is the fourth head in the lane, and the history is not about prose. Codex found that two reader-facing shell blocks ran rm -rf on a substituted path and rm -f on a record path. A how-to is executable by definition: a reader pastes it. A destructive command in a how-to is a defect with the same severity as one in the code, and worse in one respect, because it runs on a machine nobody gated.\n\nSo I will read it as an attack surface, not as a page. Concretely: that no rm, rm -f or rm -rf survives anywhere in the changed file; that any cleanup validates the exact resolved path before removing rather than merely checking existence; and that the explanatory comment attributes the safety to the comparison that actually provides it rather than to find, because a comment that credits the wrong guard is how the guard gets removed later by someone tidying up.\n\nTHE QUESTION PLANNER ASKED ME TO CHECK RATHER THAN ACCEPT. Whether resolving both sides with pwd -P is correct and necessary, or whether the comparison is weaker than it looks. Planner says the unresolved form fails the docset gate on macOS, and wants the reasoning checked rather than the symptom. I will work out what pwd -P actually normalises — symlinked path components, which is exactly the case a temporary directory on macOS hits — and whether comparing resolved forms can still admit a path the reader did not mean. A string comparison of two resolved paths is only as good as the resolution, and I want to know what it does with a trailing slash, a relative input, and a path that does not exist yet.\n\nI will run proportionate gates myself at the exact head rather than repeating the reported ones, including internal/docset with -count=1, because a cached documentation gate predates the .md edits it is meant to judge.\n\nI will record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, and file the verdict with gs review citing both artifacts, since a verdict naming fewer artifacts than the head changes paths cannot authorize the retirements a merge needs.\n\nI will also answer the framing question in the conditions: whether the stated limit about a forked sequence having no merge is honest. A how-to that overstates a limitation is a smaller defect than one that understates it, but both are the page lying to a reader who cannot check.\n\nI will not edit the head and will not merge."}